<main>
    <div class="roadmap-header fade-up fade-up-1">
        <h1>Docs</h1>
        <p class="dates">The human&rsquo;s guide to running and extending Parachute &mdash; setup, operations, and architecture. (Learning to <em>use</em> your vault day-to-day? That&rsquo;s your AI&rsquo;s playbook &mdash; the <a href="/guides/">Guides</a>.)</p>
    </div>

    <section class="section fade-up fade-up-2">
        <p>Parachute is open source. The most direct way to read about how it works is in the code itself &mdash; start with each repo's <code>README.md</code>. Below are the operator guides for running it yourself. (To set up and grow your vault, ask your connected AI to read the <strong>Getting Started</strong> note that ships inside every vault &mdash; it walks you through structuring the vault and, when you're ready, building a custom surface.) The starter notes that greet you in a new vault are also published on the open web as the <a href="/guides/">Guides</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Operator guides</h2>
        <p>Walk-throughs for running Parachute day-to-day.</p>
        <ul class="blog-list">
            <li>
                <a href="/scripting/" class="blog-post-card">
                    <h3>Scripting your vault &mdash; automate from bash, Python, or JS</h3>
                    <p class="excerpt">Your vault is an HTTP API. Mint a scoped, short-lived token with <code>parachute auth mint-token --ephemeral</code>, then read, write, and query it from any script &mdash; cron, CI, a quick Python one-liner. Plus how to think about tags, schemas, and paths so an agent (or future you) can find things later.</p>
                </a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="/multi-user/" class="blog-post-card">
                    <h3>Multi-user &mdash; host your hub for friends and family</h3>
                    <p class="excerpt">One hub, many vaults. How to provision an account per person, hand them a default password, and let them connect their own clients &mdash; without giving out admin access. The operator-facing companion to the multi-user Phase 1 design note.</p>
                </a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="/git-backup/" class="blog-post-card">
                    <h3>Git backup &mdash; back your vault up to a git repository</h3>
                    <p class="excerpt">Every change you save lands as a commit. Push to GitHub for off-machine backup, open the folder in Obsidian, or just keep a local audit trail. Configure end-to-end from the admin UI &mdash; no shell required. Operator companion to the vault-as-git-projection design note.</p>
                </a>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Per-repo READMEs</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-hub" target="_blank"><strong>parachute-hub</strong></a> &mdash; the coordinator: <code>install</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>stop</code>, <code>expose</code>, and lifecycle for every module. OAuth issuer.</li>
            <li><a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault" target="_blank"><strong>parachute-vault</strong></a> &mdash; the knowledge graph. REST + MCP, SQLite-backed. Portable-markdown export/import.</li>
            <li><a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface" target="_blank"><strong>parachute-surface</strong></a> &mdash; the UI host module. Bundles <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface/tree/main/packages/notes-ui" target="_blank">Notes</a> (an installable multi-vault PWA) as its first canonical surface; hosts additional custom SPAs under the same supervisor.</li>
            <li><a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-scribe" target="_blank"><strong>parachute-scribe</strong></a> &mdash; Whisper-compatible transcription API.</li>
            <li><a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-runner" target="_blank"><strong>parachute-runner</strong></a> &mdash; vault-as-job-substrate. Spawns <code>claude -p</code> against <code>tag:job</code> notes for owner-operated automation. <em>(exploration &mdash; shipped, not committed-core.)</em></li>
            <li><a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-patterns" target="_blank"><strong>parachute-patterns</strong></a> &mdash; cross-repo conventions (naming, schemas, OAuth scopes, ports) and the <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-patterns/tree/main/cookbook" target="_blank">cookbook</a> of worked patterns.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

